Suppose that $G$ has no atoms and that, for independent $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4$ with law $G$, $$\label{eq:minimum-moment}
 \mathbb E\!\left[\min\{X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4\}^{\,2}\right]<\infty.$$ Then, with probability one, there is no sequence $(v_i)_{i\in\mathbb Z}$ of pairwise distinct vertices with consecutive vertices adjacent and $$\sum_{k=i}^{j-1}t_{\{v_k,v_{k+1}\}}=T(v_i,v_j)
 \qquad\text{for every }i<j.$$

For every ray $p$ and all vertices $x,y$, the limit $$\label{eq:busemann-definition}
 B_p(x,y)=\lim_{j\to\infty}\bigl(T(x,p_j)-T(y,p_j)\bigr)$$ exists. It is additive and antisymmetric, satisfies $\lvert B_p(x,y)\rvert\le T(x,y)$, and is calibrated on the ray: $$B_p(p_i,p_j)=T(p_i,p_j)\qquad(i\le j).$$ Deleting a finite initial portion of a ray does not change its Busemann function. Coalescing rays have the same Busemann function. For each fixed vertex $x$, $$\label{eq:calibration-defect}
 0\le T(x,p_j)-B_p(x,p_j)\longrightarrow0.$$

Almost surely, $$\label{eq:uniform-metric}
 T(x,y)=m(y-x)+o(|x|+|y|)
 \qquad (|x|+|y|\to\infty),$$ uniformly over the two endpoints.

Let $T$ be a base metric and $T^k$ a metric obtained by increasing finitely many edge weights. Both environments are assumed to have the structural properties already established. Suppose that $k,y$ lie on the same horizontal line, with $k_1<y_1$, and that the following conditions hold for a bin $(\alpha,\beta)_I$.*

1.  *Every changed edge has both endpoints in $\{z:|z-k|_\infty\le R\}$, where $R\ge1$.*

2.  *In $T^k$ there is an oriented bigeodesic through $k$ with upper label $\rho\in(\alpha,\beta)_I$. Its arms $p^+$ and $p^-$ are contained in $Q^+(k,H_p)$ and $Q^-(k,H_p)$, respectively.*

3.  *Finite initial portions of these two arms have all their vertices in the same ball of radius $R$, and the sum of the increases along those portions is at least $b>0$.*

4.  *The base rays from $y$ of labels $\alpha$ and $-\beta$ are contained in $Q^+(y,H_c)$ and $Q^-(y,H_c)$, respectively.*


*There is a separation $S=S(s,R,H_p,H_c)$ such that $y_1-k_1>S$ implies $$\label{eq:budget-comparison}
 b\le B_\alpha(y,k)+B_{-\beta}(y,k).$$ One may take $$\label{eq:budget-separation}
 S=\max\left\{R+\frac{R+H_c}{s},\frac{H_p+H_c}{s}\right\}
 \le C_s(1+R+H_p+H_c),$$ with $C_s$ depending only on $s$. In particular, the choice of $S$ is uniform over bins and does not depend on the magnitudes of the individual increases.

Fix a bin $(\alpha,\beta)_I$, an integer spacing $l\ge1$, and a gain $b>0$. On a common probability space, mark some sites of the horizontal grid $k_j=(jl,0)$, $j\in\mathbb Z$. Suppose all sites have the same marginal probability $q_{\alpha,\beta}$ of being marked in this bin, and the base environment has its usual fixed-label Busemann asymptotics. Suppose also that for successive marks $k<y$ in the bin the comparison <a href="#eq:budget-comparison" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:budget-comparison">[eq:budget-comparison]</a> holds. In particular, this is so if the hypotheses of [lem:budget-comparison](#lem:budget-comparison) hold with common deterministic bounds and successive marks are sufficiently separated. Then $$\label{eq:label-budget}
 \frac{b}{l}\,q_{\alpha,\beta}
 \le r(\beta)-r(\alpha).$$ No independence or ergodicity assumption on the marks is needed.

There exist $a_0<b_0$ such that $$\label{eq:low-usable-probabilities}
 \begin{gathered}
 p_a=\mathbb P(t_e\le a_0)>0,
 \qquad q=\mathbb P(t_e<b_0)<\frac1{64},\\
 p_b=\mathbb P(t_e\ge b_0)=1-q>0.
 \end{gathered}$$ Call an edge *usable* if $t_e\ge b_0$, and put $\theta=8\sqrt q<1$. For each vertex $x$ and integer $n_0\ge1$, $$\label{eq:usable-fixed-site}
 \mathbb P\left(
 \begin{array}{c}
 \text{some simple path from $x$ of length $j\ge n_0$}\\
 \text{has fewer than $j/2$ usable edges}
 \end{array}\right)
 \le\frac{\theta^{n_0}}{1-\theta}.$$ If $U_n$ is the event that every simple path starting in $[-3n,3n]^2\cap\mathbb Z^2$ and having length at least $n/2$ has at least half its edges usable, then $$\label{eq:usable-box}
 \mathbb P(U_n^c)
 \le (6n+1)^2\frac{\theta^{\lceil n/2\rceil}}{1-\theta}
 \longrightarrow0.$$ Almost surely, at every fixed vertex all sufficiently long simple paths satisfy the same fraction bound.

Every individual environment $Y^{k,R}$ and every union environment $Y^{k,y,R}$ has law absolutely continuous with respect to $\mu$. There is a finite constant $C$, depending only on $s,H_0,p_a,p_b$, such that the density $L_{k,R}=d\mathcal L(Y^{k,R})/d\mu$ satisfies $$\label{eq:likelihood-bound}
 \mathbb E_\mu L_{k,R}^2\leq\exp(C\eta^2),\qquad
 \|\mathcal L(Y^{k,R})-\mu\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \leq\tfrac12\sqrt{\exp(C\eta^2)-1}.$$ The bounds are uniform in $k$ and $R$.
